  "content": {
    "id": "p11-q15-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "15",
    "prompt": "For this question, use the identity $\\cos 2\\theta=1-2\\sin^2\\theta$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p11-q15-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Let $s=\\sin 15^\\circ$. Show that $s=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2-\\sqrt3}}{2}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\dfrac{\\sqrt3}{2}=1-2s^2$, so $s^2=\\dfrac{2-\\sqrt3}{4}$. Since $s>0$, $s=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2-\\sqrt3}}{2}$.",
          "worked": "Let $\\theta=15^\\circ$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\n\\cos 30^\\circ&=1-2\\sin^2 15^\\circ\\\\\n\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}&=1-2s^2\\\\\n2s^2&=1-\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}\\\\\n2s^2&=\\frac{2-\\sqrt3}{2}\\\\\ns^2&=\\frac{2-\\sqrt3}{4}\\\\\ns&=\\frac{\\sqrt{2-\\sqrt3}}{2}\n\\end{align*}$$\nTake the positive root because $s=\\sin 15^\\circ>0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 58
      },
      {
        "id": "p11-q15-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Expand and simplify $\\dfrac{(\\sqrt3-1)^2}{2}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\dfrac{(\\sqrt3-1)^2}{2}=\\dfrac{3-2\\sqrt3+1}{2}=2-\\sqrt3$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\n\\frac{(\\sqrt3-1)^2}{2}&=\\frac{3-2\\sqrt3+1}{2}\\\\\n\\frac{(\\sqrt3-1)^2}{2}&=\\frac{4-2\\sqrt3}{2}\\\\\n\\frac{(\\sqrt3-1)^2}{2}&=2-\\sqrt3\n\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 26
      },
      {
        "id": "p11-q15-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Hence, show that $\\sin 15^\\circ=\\dfrac{\\sqrt3-1}{2\\sqrt2}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$s^2=\\dfrac{(\\sqrt3-1)^2}{8}$. Since $s>0$ and $\\sqrt3-1>0$, $\\sin 15^\\circ=\\dfrac{\\sqrt3-1}{2\\sqrt2}$.",
          "worked": "Using parts a and b,\n$$s^2=\\frac{2-\\sqrt3}{4}=\\frac{\\dfrac{(\\sqrt3-1)^2}{2}}{4}=\\frac{(\\sqrt3-1)^2}{8}.$$\nTake the positive root because $s=\\sin 15^\\circ>0$ and $\\sqrt3-1>0$.\n$$s=\\frac{\\sqrt3-1}{\\sqrt8}=\\frac{\\sqrt3-1}{2\\sqrt2}.$$\nThus $\\sin 15^\\circ=\\dfrac{\\sqrt3-1}{2\\sqrt2}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 40
      }
    ]
  },
